---
/*
  THE CART IN THE CHROME (2026-08-16, section 129) — the OWNER's call, in his
  own words: "a cart icon in the header, always visible, showing how many pieces
  are in it."

  WHY THIS IS A GLYPH AND NOT A WORD, because on this site that is the exception
  and needs saying. Everywhere else "the words are the control": the menu, the
  filters, the buy action, next and previous are all set in the label register
  with a hairline under them, and a glyph would be a foreign object. A cart is
  the one control on a shop that every visitor already recognises before reading
  anything, and it is the one control that must be findable from a photograph
  the eye is still inside. He asked for the icon. It is drawn to the site's own
  rules rather than borrowed: hairline stroke, `currentColor`, no fill, no
  badge circle, no colour — the same three rules the Instagram glyph obeys.

  IT TAKES THE CHROME'S POLARITY, not the page's, because it sits inside the
  header and the header sets `data-theme` from the measured `overlay` of the
  photograph a page opens on (SiteHeader). So it is ink on paper on a text-first
  page and paper on ink over a dark frame, with nothing painted underneath it.
  Standing rule 11 is untouched: there is no plate, no scrim and no grey.

  THE COUNT IS THE ONLY PART THAT NEEDS A SCRIPT, and the whole control is
  hidden without one — `.cart-mark` is `display: none` until `<html>` carries
  `js`, see global.css and the reasoning in
  src/pages/[lang]/cart.astro. A cart glyph that cannot count and leads to a
  page that cannot list is worse than no glyph: it is a control that lies. With
  no JavaScript the chrome is exactly what it was before this existed.

  NUMBER, NOT DOT. The count is mono, which is this site's DATA voice and is
  what a count is (tokens.css: "DATA ONLY: prices, measurements, counts"). It is
  hidden at zero rather than printing a nought, because an empty cart has
  nothing to say and a permanent 0 is furniture.
*/
import {localePath, type Locale} from "../lib/locales";
import {t} from "../i18n/ui";

interface Props {
  locale: Locale;
  class?: string;
}

const {locale, class: className = ""} = Astro.props;
---

<a
  href={localePath(locale, "cart")}
  class:list={["cart-mark", className]}
  data-cart-link
>
  {/*
    The bag: a body and a handle, drawn on the same locked 24 square the
    Instagram glyph uses so the two marks on this site share a grid. Presentation
    only — the link is named by the word beside it, and two announcements of the
    same thing is noise.
  */}
  <svg
    class="cart-glyph"
    viewBox="0 0 24 24"
    fill="none"
    stroke="currentColor"
    stroke-width="1.6"
    stroke-linecap="square"
    role="presentation"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >
    <path d="M4 8h16l-1.3 13H5.3L4 8Z" />
    <path d="M8.6 10.4V6.6a3.4 3.4 0 0 1 6.8 0v3.8" />
  </svg>
  {/*
    The word is here for anything that reads rather than looks, and the count is
    beside it so a screen reader hears "Cart 2" rather than having to hunt for
    the number. `aria-live` because the count changes under the reader's hands
    on a piece's page, without the page moving.
  */}
  <span class="sr-only">{t(locale, "cart")}</span>
  <span class="type-mono text-mono" data-cart-count aria-live="polite" hidden></span>
</a>
